<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Severe hypertension with chronic kidney disease stage G3b and severely increased albuminuria in a 59-year-old man. Please assess the cause of the kidney disease, advise on kidney-protective therapy and blood pressure targets, and share his long-term care. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Soon, within one week; kidney ultrasound and echocardiogram booked for the morning of the consultation. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is this hypertensive nephrosclerosis, or does anything point to another kidney disease or a secondary cause of hypertension that needs testing?</item><item>Do you agree with titrating losartan to the maximum dose and adding an SGLT2 inhibitor, and how much rise in creatinine should we accept?</item><item>What blood pressure target should we aim for, and which agent should come next if he stays above it?</item><item>Do his bicarbonate, PTH, and hemoglobin need treatment now?</item><item>How often should we monitor, and when should he return to nephrology?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Washington is seen at the request of Dr. Whitfield one week after presenting with headaches, palpitations, fatigue, and a blood pressure of 212/128 mmHg. On amlodipine and losartan his home readings have fallen to the 160s and 170s over 100s, he has had one mild headache this week, and his palpitations are less frequent. He has had no chest pain, breathlessness, weakness, confusion, or sudden change in vision, and no lightheadedness on standing or ankle swelling. He gets up twice a night to urinate and has seen no blood in the urine. He takes no NSAIDs or other over-the-counter products. He travels often for work and wants a plan that he can keep on the road. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Booker T. Washington was born into slavery on the James Burroughs plantation in Franklin County, Virginia, in 1856; the National Park Service timeline gives the date as April 5, 1856. [D]</item><item>Washington wrote in Up from Slavery (1901) that he was not sure of the exact date of his birth and estimated the year as 1858 or 1859; the April 5, 1856 date traces to a report by his brother John after his death and was adopted by the Tuskegee trustees. [D]</item><item>After emancipation in 1865 Washington moved with his mother, Jane, and his family to Malden, West Virginia, where he worked in the salt and coal mines; a National Park Service release gives his mother&apos;s death as 1876. [D]</item><item>Washington opened Tuskegee Institute in Tuskegee, Alabama, on July 4, 1881, at age 25, and led it for the rest of his life; in March 1915 he sponsored National Negro Health Week. [D]</item><item>In 1911 Washington was beaten in New York City and received two large gashes in his head and a torn ear, recovering without apparent sequelae; he had chronic indigestion, especially when traveling, and spent several days in 1911 at the Battle Creek Sanitarium. [D]</item></list><paragraph>5 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

