    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Chronic kidney disease stage G3b A3 (N18.32). Small, echogenic, symmetric kidneys with severe hypertension, left ventricular hypertrophy, and hypertensive retinopathy fit long-standing hypertensive nephrosclerosis, and the disease is chronic, not acute. Albuminuria of 820 mg/g is high for nephrosclerosis alone, so the response to treatment is the test: a fall of at least 30 percent supports the diagnosis. One week after losartan, creatinine is 2.2 mg/dL (up 5 percent), eGFR 34, and potassium 4.6 mmol/L, so blockade can be pushed further. His risk of progression is very high. [S]</item><item>Severe hypertension with hypertensive kidney disease (I12.9). 172/104 mmHg, improving but far above target. The symmetric kidneys, the absence of an abdominal bruit, and the small creatinine rise on losartan argue against renal artery stenosis, and his potassium is normal. Secondary causes need testing only if blood pressure stays above target on three agents including a diuretic. [S]</item><item>Mild anemia of chronic kidney disease (D63.1). Hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL with ferritin 210 ng/mL and transferrin saturation 26 percent: iron replete, and well above any threshold for treatment. [S]</item><item>Mineral and bone disorder and acid-base status of chronic kidney disease. PTH 92 pg/mL is mildly raised with normal calcium and phosphate, and bicarbonate 21 to 22 mmol/L is above the level that needs alkali. Neither needs treatment now. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Today&apos;s kidney ultrasound: both kidneys small (9.4 and 9.6 cm) and echogenic, symmetric, without hydronephrosis. Today&apos;s echocardiogram: concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, ejection fraction 60 percent. Last week&apos;s ECG and dilated funduscopic examination. Both basic metabolic panels, the urine albumin-creatinine ratio, mineral studies, blood count, and iron studies. No urinalysis with microscopy is on file. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
